  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nPredominantly overcast skies prevailed across the area thus far \ntoday, including pockets of lingering fog and drizzle to light rain. \nBy early afternoon, visibilities have improved to greater than 6 \nmiles in most locations and stratus has begun to go more cellular in \nnature. This is especially so in areas where higher level cloud \ncover has understandably been non-existent or clearing out. Of \ncasual note, areas where mixing has been more robust, tending to be \nsouth of I-70, have noted the potential within CAM soundings for \nweak instability underneath the inversion to develop which could \nlead to some very weakly convective light rain / showers. This \npotential has most recently begun to be seen on KEAX radar along to \njust on the SGF side of the CWA border, including around and east of \nClinton and south of Sedalia. Given that, do expect to see some \nadditional activity south of I-70 where more robust cellular action \nis noted on visible satellite. Would not do much more than getting \nthe ground wet or ticking off up to a couple or few hundredths of an \ninch of rain. \n\nDeterministic and ensemble synoptic scale guidance have shown little \nnotable changes with recent runs. There remains fair to good \nagreement on the general evolution of the synoptic pattern through \nlate week. This includes a weakly closed to open mid-level wave \ndrifting eastward across the Northern Plains today and Tuesday, \ngradually developing into a messy Rex Block-like pattern, and \nfinally a closed low over and off the East Coast by late week into \nnext weekend. And on its heels will be ridging working back into the \ncentral CONUS, but looks to struggle to make too much direct \nprogress over the area given the East Coast blockages. All the \nwhile, a large surface high centered over the northern Great Lakes \ninto southern Ontario. \n\nWhat does that all mean for the immediate area? Temperature wise, it \nresults in pretty persistent fall-like temperatures as NE to E \nsurface/near-surface flow prevails throughout the week. This results \nin highs in the 60s/70s and lows generally in the upper 40s to 50s. \nCoolest lows tending to be mid-week when cloud cover should be least \nprevalent. Cloud cover and precipitation wise, yields lingering \nperiods of stratus and drizzle to light rain chances through Tuesday \nwith best chances over northern areas closer to the passing mid-\nlevel wave. Followed by a dry mid-week as mid-level height rises, \nthe aforementioned ridging, slides into the central CONUS. Late week \nis where the most uncertainty resides as the degree and position of \nthe blocking pattern over the East Coast will dictate how far east \nprecipitation chances creep and how quickly the area may see warming \ntemperatures. For now, not much change from previous forecast cycle \nwith the prevailing consensus keeping the mid-level ridge axis just \nto the west of the area and embedded shortwaves rounding the ridge \ninto Nebraska/Iowa. Within the gridded forecast, this yields 20 to \n40 percent PoPs beginning to creep into NW/W Missouri and NE Kansas \nThursday afternoon and evening and creeping eastward through Friday. \nTakeaway being chances increase as you work toward W/NW Missouri and \ninto Kansas. Similar may occur into the weekend with deterministic \nguidance showing additional shortwaves within the flow, possibly \nattempting to flatten the ridge some. Understandably, aside from \nPoPs, this also results in the most uncertainty in temperatures with \nNBM spreads increasing to the 8 to 12 deg F range Saturday. \n\nRegardless of all of the above, hope most are able to enjoy a \nstretch of cooler temperatures after the stretch of late \nsummer/early fall heat.",
